SmartPrep Station (SPS): An Intelligent Pre‑Cooking Food Preparation System for Next‑Generation Kitchens

Design, Formalisation, and Simulation‑Based Feasibility Study

Ouadi Maakoul

AI brothers: Deepseek,Grok XAi, chatGpt 

Abstract

Food preparation—washing, peeling, cutting, weighing, and transferring ingredients—remains the most labour‑intensive and least automated stage of domestic and light‑commercial cooking. While cooking appliances and smart kitchen ecosystems have advanced considerably, the tasks that precede heating are still performed almost entirely by hand. This dissertation proposes, formalises, and evaluates via high‑fidelity simulation the SmartPrep Station (SPS), a modular, under‑countertop, AI‑driven appliance designed to automate the complete pre‑cooking pipeline while preserving human creativity and nutritional oversight.

We introduce a multi‑objective optimal control framework that jointly considers preparation time, physical effort, food waste, and preparation quality under realistic domestic constraints. The system architecture integrates computer vision, adaptive mechatronic modules, and a real‑time scheduling engine, all constrained by residential safety, hygiene, and energy requirements. The present work develops the mathematical and engineering foundations, specifies a complete system design, and uses a digital twin in the NVIDIA Isaac Sim environment to predict performance. No physical prototype has yet been constructed; all reported metrics are simulation‑based and should be interpreted as feasibility projections.

The simulation results suggest that the SPS concept can reduce preparation time by a factor of approximately 2.4 relative to manual benchmarks, while achieving peel waste below 7 % and cut uniformity within ±0.5 mm. The study identifies key technical risks, proposes a phased prototyping and validation roadmap, and discusses the regulatory, market, and societal implications of bringing such a device to market. The SPS demonstrates that a mathematically rigorous, user‑centred approach to kitchen automation can be formally described and simulated, providing a credible foundation for the next generation of intelligent kitchen assistants.

