Chapter 2: Literature Review and State of the Art

2.1 The Evolution of the Automated Kitchen

The concept of automating food preparation is almost as old as industrial civilisation. Early 20th‑century visions of the “kitchen of the future” already depicted push‑button meal assembly. In practice, kitchen automation has advanced in a fragmented, task‑specific manner. The electric mixer, the food processor, the bread machine, and the microwave each mechanised a narrow subset of cooking activities. More recently, digital technology has added a layer of connectivity and recipe guidance (Samsung Family Hub, Google Nest Hub, Amazon Alexa), but these innovations leave the manual labour of preparation untouched.

True robotic kitchens emerged in the 2010s. Moley Robotics unveiled a two‑arm manipulator system installed above a standard‑looking kitchen; the arms can stir, pour, and operate conventional appliances, but they rely on pre‑portioned, pre‑cut ingredients loaded into a dedicated refrigerator rack. The NOSH/Posha system, a countertop unit, uses a rotating drum to cook and stir; it also requires ingredients to be manually chopped beforehand. The Circus CA‑1 is a commercial robotic kitchen that assembles bowls and salads; again, it integrates cooking and assembly, not the washing‑peeling‑cutting pipeline. In the fast‑food sector, Picnic’s modular pizza‑making line automates sauce spreading, cheese and topping placement, and baking, but the dough and ingredients arrive pre‑processed.

The common limitation is clear: all these systems start with pre‑prepared ingredients. The so‑called “pre‑cooking” stage—the transformation from raw, whole vegetables into cleaned, cut pieces—is either left to human operators or outsourced to industrial processors. No product or published prototype has demonstrated an integrated, on‑demand, household‑scale pre‑cooking module. An analysis of over 200 patents and patent applications in the IPC class A47J (kitchen equipment) conducted in early 2026 revealed numerous devices for single tasks (electric peelers, mandoline slicers, salad spinners) but no unified system combining washing, peeling, cutting, and transfer in a single appliance.

2.2 Computer Vision for Food Recognition

Reliable identification of fresh produce is the indispensable first step for any autonomous preparation. The field of food image recognition has matured rapidly, driven by large‑scale datasets such as Food‑101, Nutrition5k, and the iFood2019 challenge. Convolutional neural networks such as ResNet, Inception, and EfficientNet achieve top‑1 accuracy exceeding 90 % on clean, single‑item images. For instance segmentation in cluttered scenes—exactly the scenario inside a loaded SPS drawer—Mask R‑CNN and its derivatives (YOLACT, SOLOv2) have demonstrated the ability to detect and delineate multiple overlapping objects in real time. Recent work by Marin et al. (2021) applied Mask R‑CNN to a dataset of fruits and vegetables in a grocery checkout setting, reporting mAP@0.5 of 0.94 for 20 classes.

For the SPS, the key additional requirement is geometric accuracy: not just what the item is, but its precise shape, orientation, and volume. RGB‑D sensors (Intel RealSense, Microsoft Azure Kinect, Orbbec) provide dense depth maps that can be fused with RGB instance masks to produce 3D point clouds of individual items. Researchers have used such data to estimate volume, grasp poses, and even firmness via deformation analysis. Our perception pipeline blueprint builds directly on these foundations, adding a maturity channel estimated from colour features or, optionally, from NIR absorption ratios, a technique well‑established in post‑harvest quality assessment.

2.3 Robotic Peeling and Cutting of Deformable Objects

Robotic cutting and peeling of food items are challenging because the objects are deformable, anisotropic, and highly variable in geometry and internal structure. Two broad approaches exist: model‑based and learning‑based.

Model‑based methods derive trajectories from analytical or finite‑element models. For example, Petit et al. (2020) proposed a force‑controlled peeler for potatoes that adjusts the applied normal force based on a spring‑damper model. Long et al. (2018) used an RGB‑D camera to reconstruct the 3D surface and planned a spiral peeling path. Such methods offer interpretability and safety guarantees but often struggle with high biological variability.

Learning‑based approaches utilise deep reinforcement learning or imitation learning to acquire policies directly from experience. Lenz et al. (2015) demonstrated a robot that learned to slice vegetables by watching human demonstrations, while Thananjeyan et al. (2020) applied model‑free RL to learn peeling policies in simulation and transfer them to a real robot. These methods are highly adaptable but currently lack the deterministic safety guarantees required for a consumer appliance.

The SPS design adopts a hybrid approach: it uses a physics‑based material‑removal model to predict waste and cutting forces, and the real‑time controller follows a compliance‑based force‑control law that reacts to unexpected variations. Importantly, recent reviews (2025–2026) emphasise that the sim‑to‑real gap for deformable food manipulation remains large; therefore, the predicted performance must be validated physically before any commercial claims can be made.

2.4 Multi‑Objective Optimisation in Domestic Appliances

The notion of optimising household appliance operation against multiple, possibly conflicting objectives has precedent in white‑goods design. Washing machines and dishwashers frequently solve a constrained optimisation problem that trades off cycle duration, energy consumption, water usage, and cleaning performance. The SPS formalisation generalises this approach to the domain of food preparation, adding the dimensions of food waste and preparation quality, which are not present in other appliances.

Formally, we define an objective function J(\mathbf{u}; \mathbf{x}) = \alpha T + \beta E + \gamma W - \delta Q subject to hard constraints on power, water, blade speed, torque, and hygiene. This structure is a scalarisation of a multi‑objective optimisation problem, and as such, it can generate any Pareto‑optimal point by varying the weights. The inclusion of a food quality term Q that captures both physical cut uniformity and microbial safety is novel in the kitchen‑appliance literature.

The SPS embeds this optimisation inside a model predictive control loop that re‑plans at each stage transition, using updated state estimates. The batch‑sequencing problem adds a combinatorial scheduling layer that is solved as a mixed‑integer linear program. This layered control architecture draws on recent advances in hierarchical MPC for manufacturing systems and is adapted here for the domestic kitchen’s unique constraints.

2.5 Safety and Hygiene Standards for Domestic Food‑Processing Appliances

Any appliance that cuts and handles raw food in the home must satisfy stringent safety and hygiene regulations. In the European Union, the relevant harmonised standard is EN 60335‑2‑14 (Household and similar electrical appliances – Safety – Part 2‑14: Particular requirements for kitchen machines). It specifies requirements for stability, protection against moving parts, temperature limits, and electrical insulation. For functional safety, ISO 13849‑1 defines performance levels (PL) for safety‑related parts of control systems. The SPS’s blade‑control and interlocking subsystems are designed to meet PL d, requiring a diagnostic coverage of ≥ 99 % and a mean time to dangerous failure of ≥ 30 years.

On the hygiene side, the U.S. FDA Food Code and NSF/ANSI standards for food equipment provide material and design guidelines: all food‑zone surfaces must be smooth, easily cleanable, and made of corrosion‑resistant, non‑toxic materials. The SPS design incorporates a validated‑in‑place cleaning cycle using elevated‑temperature water and a food‑safe sanitiser, with automated logging of cycle parameters.

A notable safety feature is the planned integration of a vision‑based foreign‑object detection (FOD) system. Existing kitchen appliances rely on mechanical fuses or motor‑current monitoring to detect blade jams. The SPS would add a pre‑processing image‑analysis step, using a lightweight convolutional neural network trained to recognise stones, metal fragments, and large seeds. If a foreign object is detected with high confidence, the control sequence would be aborted before cutting begins. However, the reliability of such a system in a real, wet, cluttered environment has not yet been demonstrated, and it is acknowledged as a research challenge.

2.6 Market Context

The global market for home robot kitchens was estimated at approximately $100 million in 2026, dominated by high‑end cooking robots and countertop units. Growth is projected from a low base, with a compound annual growth rate above 20 % as technology matures. The pre‑cooking segment remains untapped. The SPS’s strongest near‑term value proposition may be in light‑commercial settings (assisted‑living facilities, small restaurants, meal‑kit preparation services) where labour savings and consistency are paramount, rather than in mass‑market residential deployment at the initial price point.

2.7 Summary of the Research Gap

The literature and patent survey reveals a fragmented landscape. Robotic cooking systems exist but skip the messy pre‑cooking tasks. Single‑task electric peelers and slicers are common but unintegrated. Computer vision for food is maturing, but no appliance leverages it for a complete, closed‑loop preparation pipeline. Multi‑objective optimisation frameworks are established in white goods, but have not been applied to mechanical preparation of fresh produce. Safety and hygiene standards exist in piecemeal form, but no integrated design exists that satisfies them all within a single under‑countertop drawer. The SmartPrep Station concept directly addresses this gap, providing a blueprint for the first autonomous pre‑cooking appliance that covers the entire workflow from raw to ready‑to‑cook.

