Chapter 3: Mathematical Formulation and System Architecture

3.1 Introduction

This chapter formalises the SmartPrep Station as an integrated, under‑countertop, autonomous food‑preparation system. While the preceding chapters established the motivation and surveyed related work, here we develop the complete mathematical framework that governs the system’s operation and the architectural blueprint that realises it. The design is guided by the central hypothesis that a unified optimisation of preparation time, food waste, and preparation quality, subject to rigorously defined safety and resource constraints, yields a practical and socially beneficial kitchen robot. We present the system as a cyber‑physical loop: a perception module estimates the state of ingredients, a decision engine solves a constrained multi‑objective optimal control problem, and a set of interchangeable mechatronic modules execute the resulting instructions under continuous safety supervision.

3.2 System Architecture Overview

The SPS is conceptually housed in a drawer that slides beneath a standard kitchen countertop, utilising a form factor of width ≤ 60 cm, depth ≤ 55 cm, and height ≤ 25 cm to remain compatible with existing cabinetry. The functional architecture comprises the following subsystems:

1. Ingress and containment drawer – accepts raw ingredients and seals the working volume.
2. Perception module – an array of RGB‑D cameras, a load cell, and an optional near‑infrared (NIR) point spectrometer.
3. Washing and sanitisation chamber – recirculating water jets, a rotating basket, and a UV‑C LED array for microbial reduction.
4. Adaptive peeling station – a servoed, pressure‑controlled abrasive roller or blade‑based mechanism with compliance sensing.
5. Modular cutting system – a tool‑changer carousel hosting cassettes for slicing, dicing, julienne, grating, and future special functions.
6. Precision weighing platform – located directly beneath the active preparation zone to provide real‑time mass feedback.
7. Waste collection and separation – dedicated bin for peels and trimmings, with automated compaction and sealed storage.
8. Transfer mechanism – a linear actuator with a food‑grade gripper that moves the ingredient through stations and deposits it into a user‑provided cooking vessel.
9. Embedded control unit (ECU) – a heterogeneous compute platform (ARM Cortex‑A + FPGA + safety MCU) running AI inference, the optimiser, and real‑time motor controllers.
10. Human‑machine interface – touchscreen, voice control, and an application programming interface (API) for smart home integration via Matter and Wi‑Fi.

All subsystems communicate over a real‑time Ethernet backbone (EtherCAT) with strict cycle times of 1 ms for motion control and 100 ms for perception updates. A block diagram of this architecture is provided in Appendix B.

3.3 Mathematical Formulation of the Optimal Preparation Problem

The SPS is designed to select and execute a sequence of operations that optimally trade off multiple, often conflicting, objectives. We model the preparation of a single ingredient as a parameterised dynamical process and then extend the formulation to a sequence of items.

3.3.1 State‑Space Representation of Ingredients

Let the state of a raw ingredient be a vector

\mathbf{x} = (c,\; \mathbf{s},\; w,\; m)

where

· c \in \mathcal{C} is a discrete food category (e.g., potato, carrot, onion, apple), encoded as a one‑hot vector of dimension |\mathcal{C}| = N_c.
· \mathbf{s} \in \mathbb{R}^3 describes the bounding‑box dimensions (length, width, height) derived from the RGB‑D point cloud, to which a convex‑hull volume and shape descriptors are added for irregular items.
· w \in \mathbb{R}^+ is the mass measured by the load cell (with 0.1 g resolution).
· m \in [0, 1] is a continuous maturity/ripeness index, estimated from the RGB spectrum and, if available, NIR absorbance ratios.

The perception system provides a noisy estimate \hat{\mathbf{x}} with an associated covariance matrix \mathbf{P}_x, obtained from a Bayesian fusion of camera, weight, and spectral data.

3.3.2 Multi‑Objective Cost Function and Constraints

For a single ingredient, the SPS selects a control vector

\mathbf{u} = (t_{\text{wash}},\, \tau_{\text{peel}},\, g_{\text{cut}},\, v_{\text{blade}},\, \text{traj})

where t_{\text{wash}} is washing duration, \tau_{\text{peel}} is the peeling torque/time profile, g_{\text{cut}} defines the cutting geometry (slice thickness d, number of cuts, pattern), v_{\text{blade}} is the linear speed of the cutting blade, and \text{traj} is the transfer path.

The overall performance of the operation is evaluated by a scalar objective function

J(\mathbf{u}; \mathbf{x}) = \alpha T(\mathbf{u}; \mathbf{x}) + \beta E(\mathbf{u}) + \gamma W(\mathbf{u}; \mathbf{x}) - \delta Q(\mathbf{u}; \mathbf{x})

where

· T [s] is the total cycle time for this ingredient,
· E [arbitrary units] quantifies the physical effort required from the human user. In the fully automated mode targeted by this study, the user is only required to load and unload ingredients; therefore E \equiv 0 and this term is inactive. The parameter \beta is retained to allow future assist‑mode variants where the user may manually feed certain steps,
· W [g] is the mass of edible tissue removed during peeling and trimming (food waste),
· Q [dimensionless] is a composite quality metric: Q = w_1 Q_{\text{uniformity}} + w_2 Q_{\text{hygiene}}, where Q_{\text{uniformity}} = 1 - (\sigma_d / d_{\text{target}}) (bounded in [0,1]) and Q_{\text{hygiene}} is the microbial log‑reduction normalised to a [0,1] scale, with 0 corresponding to no reduction and 1 to a target 3‑log reduction or better.

The positive coefficients \alpha, \beta, \gamma, \delta are user‑adjustable weights that reflect a desired trade‑off; they can be set via a simple slider (e.g., “speed vs. waste reduction”). In the fully autonomous mode, the system minimises J subject to a set of hard constraints:

\begin{aligned}
& 0 \leq v_{\text{blade}} \leq v_{\max} \quad \text{(blade speed safety limit)} \\
& \tau_{\text{peel}} \leq \tau_{\max} \quad \text{(motor torque to avoid crushing)} \\
& V_{\text{water}} \leq V_{\max} \quad \text{(water consumption ≤ 2.0 L per cycle)} \\
& P_{\text{total}} \leq P_{\max} \quad \text{(total power ≤ 1500 W)} \\
& \text{Microbial load after prep} \leq 10^2\; \text{CFU/g} \quad \text{(hygiene requirement)}
\end{aligned}

The resulting optimisation is a nonlinear program (NLP) with both continuous and integer variables (the choice of g_{\text{cut}} is discrete). Because the cycle time T and waste W are computed via physics‑based models, the optimal \mathbf{u}^* can be found offline for each state \mathbf{x} and stored in a lookup table, or solved in real time using a Model Predictive Control (MPC) scheme with a simplified dynamics model.

3.3.3 Sequencing and Overall Workflow Optimisation

When a batch of N ingredients with states \mathbf{x}_1, \dots, \mathbf{x}_N is placed in the drawer, the system must also solve a scheduling problem to minimise total preparation time while respecting module usage conflicts. Let the sequence be a permutation \sigma of \{1, \dots, N\}. Between two consecutive items, a setup time s_{ij} is required if a tool change (e.g., switching from dicing cassette to julienne) is needed. The total makespan is

T_{\text{total}} = \sum_{k=1}^{N} T_{\sigma(k)} + \sum_{k=1}^{N-1} s_{\sigma(k), \sigma(k+1)}

where T_i is the optimal preparation time for item i as determined from the single‑ingredient optimisation. The SPS solves a mixed‑integer linear programming (MILP) problem with an objective that combines total time and tool changes, minimising:

\min_{\sigma} \left( T_{\text{total}} + \lambda \sum_{k=1}^{N-1} \mathbb{I}[\text{tool change at step } k] \right)

subject to the individual ingredient constraints. For a small N (typical home use, N \leq 10), the problem is solvable to optimality by branch‑and‑bound on the embedded ECU in under 100 ms. This scheduling layer is a key differentiation from single‑task kitchen robots.

3.4 Perception System Blueprint

The perception pipeline is designed to estimate \hat{\mathbf{x}} in three stages:

1. Instance segmentation and classification: An EfficientDet‑D4 backbone, quantised to INT8, would run on a dedicated neural processing unit (NPU) to identify and localise each item. The model would require fine‑tuning on a custom dataset of cluttered kitchen scenes (see Chapter 6 for dataset plan).
2. Geometric reconstruction: The depth channel provides a 3D point cloud, from which the oriented bounding box \mathbf{s} is extracted. For irregular items, a convex hull and equivalent ellipsoid moments are used.
3. Mass and maturity: The load cell measurement w is fused with the volume estimate from the depth camera via an extended Kalman filter to provide a robust density estimate. Maturity m would be estimated using a lightweight regression model on RGB colour histograms and, if the NIR sensor is enabled, absorbance ratios. The model would be calibrated against standard ripeness indices.

The full state estimate is \hat{\mathbf{x}} = (c, \mathbf{s}, w, m) with an uncertainty measure used by the control policy to adopt a cautious strategy when confidence is low (e.g., thicker peel removal for uncertain maturity). Real‑world training data are not yet available; the simulation study (Chapter 5) uses synthetic data to assess feasibility.

3.5 Control and Safety Architecture

The control system employs a hierarchical structure:

· High‑level scheduler: MILP batch sequencing and user intent parsing.
· Mid‑level MPC: Single‑item optimisation using the current state estimate and user‑selected weights.
· Low‑level real‑time control: PID force‑control for peeling, synchronised feed‑to‑blade speed for cutting, and jerk‑limited trajectories for transfer.

Safety is architected as an independent channel on a dedicated safety MCU (Texas Instruments Hercules RM57L, dual‑core lockstep). Critical functions include: electromagnetic door interlock, redundant speed sensing, force/torque monitoring against safe envelopes, and a vision‑based foreign‑object detector (MobileNetV3‑based) planned to run on the safety MCU. In the event of any safety violation, an emergency stop is triggered within 10 ms. The design targets SIL 2 / PL d per ISO 13849‑1, but formal certification would require physical testing with a notified body.

3.6 Conclusion

This chapter has established the mathematical and architectural core of the SPS. The multi‑objective formulation captures the essential trade‑offs of domestic food preparation, and the modular architecture ensures that each subsystem can be independently specified and, eventually, prototyped and tested. The next chapter translates this architecture into a detailed engineering specification ready for future fabrication.

