Chapter 4: Engineering Design Specification

4.1 Design Philosophy and Requirements Traceability

This chapter presents a comprehensive engineering specification for the SPS. No physical prototype has yet been fabricated; the document is intended as a build‑ready blueprint. Every design decision is traceable to the objective function J, the constraints, or the safety and hygiene requirements identified in Chapter 3. The guiding principles are:

1. Modularity – all major functional blocks are independent, interchangeable, and individually testable.
2. Residential compatibility – the entire system fits within a standard 60 cm under‑counter drawer envelope, operates on a single‑phase domestic supply, and consumes no more than 2 L of water per cycle.
3. Safety‑by‑design – an independent safety controller and physical interlocks ensure a SIL 2 equivalent integrity level, as demanded by IEC 60335‑2‑14 and ISO 13849‑1.
4. Accessibility – the user interface and automated workflow remove the need for sustained fine‑motor manipulation or prolonged standing.

4.2 Mechanical Design

4.2.1 Chassis and Enclosure

The load‑bearing chassis is a welded frame of 304 stainless steel (1.5 mm thick) with external dimensions 595 × 530 × 240 mm, fitting a standard 600 mm European cabinet. Full‑extension telescopic slides (rated 50 kg) support the drawer, and an electromagnetic solenoid lock (24 V, holding force 200 N) prevents opening when moving parts are active. A food‑grade silicone gasket and a slight positive‑pressure air curtain (HEPA‑filtered, 12 V fan) isolate the internal atmosphere.

4.2.2 Washing and Sanitisation Chamber

A cylindrical drum (316L stainless steel, ∅220 mm × 280 mm) rotates on bearings driven by a brushless DC motor (Maxon EC 90 flat, 90 W) via a toothed belt. Internal helical ribs tumble the produce. Three high‑pressure jets (diaphragm pump, 12 V, 5 L min⁻¹, 8 bar) deliver filtered water from a 2 L reservoir. A UV‑C LED module (275 nm, 50 mW, Seoul Viosys) is mounted on the drum lid. A custom electrolysis cell generates 20 ppm hypochlorous acid on‑demand. The water recirculates through a 100 µm mesh and a three‑way valve enables discharge. Total water consumption is projected to remain below 2 L cycle⁻¹.

4.2.3 Peeling Station

Two counter‑rotating abrasive rollers (silicon carbide #80, ∅40 mm × 200 mm) form the primary peeler. One roller is stationary; the other is mounted on a linear slide actuated by a stepper motor (NEMA 17, 0.45 N m) driving a lead screw (pitch 2 mm). A strain‑gauge load cell (Futek LSB200, 10 N) measures normal force, enabling a PID controller to maintain a prescribed force profile F_d derived from the optimisation policy. An alternative blade‑peeling head, consisting of a spring‑loaded carbide blade, is stored in the cutting‑tool magazine and can be exchanged automatically for delicate items. The material‑removal model W_{\text{peel}} = \rho_c \cdot A_{\text{peel}} \cdot d_{\text{peel}} determines the predicted waste, with parameters \rho_c calibrated from published food‑engineering data.

4.2.4 Cutting System and Cassette Magazine

A Cartesian gantry (X–Z axes) positions the ingredient against the active cutting tool. The X‑axis (stroke 300 mm, belt drive, NEMA 23) moves a pusher plate, while the Z‑axis (lead screw, 200 mm) lifts the tool into engagement. A rotary magazine (6 positions, Geneva drive) stores the cassettes:

· C1: Slicing disc (gap adjustable 1.0–15.0 mm)
· C2: Dicing grid (5/8/10 mm interchangeable plates)
· C3: Julienne comb (3×3 mm, 5×5 mm)
· C4: Grating drum (2 mm teeth)
· C5: Herb stripper (toothed rollers)
· C6: Coring/slicing mandrel (apple/pear)

Cassette selection and docking are performed by a pneumatic cylinder (4 bar). The main slicing disc is spun by a brushless DC motor (500–2000 RPM). Blade linear speed is limited to 2 m s⁻¹; feed rate is synchronised to achieve target thickness d with a modelled tolerance of ±0.5 mm. The control law governing g_{\text{cut}} and v_{\text{blade}} is the minimiser of the local cost function, implemented as a look‑up table indexed by \hat{\mathbf{x}}.

4.2.5 Transfer Robot and Weighing

A dual‑axis Cartesian robot (X: 400 mm, Z: 150 mm, Igus drylin actuators) carries a soft pneumatic gripper (silicone, Shore A 30). The gripper closes with 0.5 bar pressure, sufficient to hold a 500 g vegetable without bruising. A strain‑gauge platform (HBM PW10A, 5 kg, class C3) is integrated into the floor of the preparation chamber and is read by a 24‑bit ADC at 80 Hz. Transfer trajectories are fifth‑order polynomials generated by a jerk‑limited path planner, with obstacle avoidance based on a static 2D occupancy grid.

4.3 Perception Subsystem Specification

An Intel RealSense D435i RGB‑D camera is mounted in the ceiling, providing 1280 × 720 RGB and depth images at 30 fps. The depth map is registered to the RGB frame and converted to a point cloud using factory calibration. A miniature NIR spectrometer (Hamamatsu C12880MA, 340–850 nm) points at the centre of the drawer via a fibre‑optic probe; it is used only when the maturity channel is activated. The load cell measurement is fused with the volume estimate from the depth data in an extended Kalman filter to produce \hat{\mathbf{x}} with associated covariance \mathbf{P}_x.

The vision pipeline is designed to run an EfficientDet‑D4 instance segmentation network, quantised to INT8, on a dedicated NPU. The model would be fine‑tuned on a dataset of cluttered vegetable and fruit images; the simulation study (Chapter 5) assumes a proxy synthetic dataset, and a plan for collecting real training data is outlined in Chapter 6.

4.4 Control System and Software Architecture

A heterogeneous embedded platform is specified:

· Perception and planning: NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX (8 GB) – runs ROS 2 Humble, the segmentation network, the MPC policy, and the batch scheduler.
· Real‑time motion control: Xilinx Zynq‑7020 FPGA implementing EtherCAT master, PID loops, and trajectory interpolation at 1 kHz.
· Safety controller: Texas Instruments Hercules RM57L (dual‑core lockstep ARM Cortex‑R) – monitors interlocks, force limits, and the vision‑based foreign‑object detector.

All subsystems communicate via EtherCAT. The perception node publishes state estimates at 10 Hz. The optimisation layer is implemented as a look‑up table for single‑item control, while batch scheduling uses the OR‑Tools MILP solver. The user interface consists of a 7‑inch touchscreen (Nextion) and an offline voice‑recognition engine (Picovoice, wake word “Hey SPS”). Matter 1.2 connectivity provides integration with external smart‑home ecosystems.

4.5 Safety and Hygiene Design

Safety is architected as an independent SIL 2 channel. Critical functions include:

· Electromagnetic door lock: engaged whenever moving parts are powered; release permitted only after all actuators have stopped and a 2‑second delay has elapsed, verified by redundant Hall sensors.
· Force/torque monitoring: the safety MCU reads the load cell and motor current sensors; if the force exceeds a pre‑computed safe envelope by 20 %, an emergency stop is triggered.
· Vision‑based foreign‑object detection: a lightweight convolutional network (MobileNetV3‑based) is planned to run on the safety MCU, trained on images of stones, metal fragments, and large seeds. If a foreign object is detected with confidence > 95 %, the sequence is aborted before cutting. Important caveat: This system has not been tested on real, wet produce with realistic foreign objects; its reliability in a production appliance remains to be established.

Hygiene is ensured by material choice (all food‑contact surfaces are 316L stainless steel or FDA‑compliant elastomer), a self‑cleaning cycle (70 °C water + cleaning agent circulated for 5 min, followed by UV‑C), and a tool‑free design for manual deep cleaning. The expected microbial reduction, based on literature data for 20 ppm HOCl and UV‑C, is > 3 log for E. coli and Listeria.

4.6 Integration and Communication

A RESTful API over Wi‑Fi 6 and a Matter 1.2 endpoint expose the SPS’s capabilities to external recipe apps and smart displays. The interface allows a user to select a recipe, which is then decomposed into a sequence of pre‑cooking operations. A companion mobile application is planned to provide a “SmartPrep” recipe database, with meals designed to be compatible with the SPS’s cut modes.

4.7 Bill of Materials and Cost Projection

The projected bill of materials for a production run of 10 000 units is estimated at approximately €1 150, based on quotations for comparable components. Major cost drivers include the Jetson Orin NX (€450 in volume), the RGB‑D camera (€180), stainless‑steel fabrication (€120), and the tool magazine assembly (€100). Target retail price is under €2 500, positioning the SPS in the premium built‑in appliance segment. It must be emphasised that this is a preliminary estimate; actual manufacturing, assembly, certification, and software costs could significantly alter the final price. A detailed cost analysis with a manufacturer is an essential next step.

