Chapter 5: Simulation‑Based Feasibility Study

5.1 Simulation Strategy

In the absence of a physical prototype, a digital twin of the SPS has been constructed in the NVIDIA Isaac Sim robotics simulator. This chapter reports a series of simulation experiments designed to predict the system’s performance against the objectives defined in Chapter 3 and to verify that the architectural specifications are internally consistent and potentially capable of meeting the design targets. All results are simulation‑based; they use physics models and sensor noise characteristics calibrated from literature and from manufacturer datasheets, and they should be interpreted as projections, not as achieved performance.

5.2 Simulation Environment

The digital twin replicates the full kinematic chain of the SPS: drawer, rotating wash drum, peeling rollers, cutting gantry, tool magazine, transfer Cartesian, and gripper. Deformable food items are modelled using finite‑element‑method surrogates. Material properties (Young’s modulus, Poisson’s ratio, fracture toughness) for potato, carrot, onion, and apple are taken from published food‑engineering compendia (e.g., Mohsenin, 1986; ASABE standards). The perception pipeline is emulated by a synthetic data generator that produces RGB‑D images with physically based noise and varying levels of occlusion, then processes them through a pre‑trained EfficientDet‑D4 model (trained on a proxy dataset of 50 k synthetic images) to obtain state estimates with realistic uncertainty.

5.3 Subsystem‑Level Simulation Results

5.3.1 Peeling Waste and Time

The abrasive peeling process was simulated for 500 virtual potatoes, carrots, and apples, each with randomised geometry and surface roughness. The force‑control law maintained a mean contact force of 1.2 N (SD 0.15 N). The predicted peel waste mass, expressed as a percentage of total mass, was: potato 6.5 % (SD 0.9 %), carrot 5.3 % (SD 0.8 %), apple 4.8 % (SD 0.7 %). Peel cycle time per item averaged 20.1 s (potato), 14.7 s (carrot), 12.3 s (apple). These values are comparable to published results for manual peeling and are within the design targets.

5.3.2 Cutting Accuracy

The dicing module was simulated with 200 virtual carrots (target 10 mm cube). The slicing disc and pusher feed‑rate control yielded a mean cube‑side of 10.02 mm with a standard deviation of 0.36 mm, inside the ±0.5 mm specification. For julienne cuts (5 mm × 5 mm), the standard deviation increased to 0.42 mm but remained within tolerance.

5.3.3 Washing Hygiene

The wash cycle was simulated using a computational fluid dynamics (CFD) model of the rotating drum, validated against published particle‑removal studies. The addition of 20 ppm HOCl and UV‑C exposure of 90 s was estimated to achieve a 4.1‑log reduction in bacterial load, based on kinetic models from food microbiology literature. The model predicts that the post‑prep total viable count will be below 10 CFU g⁻¹ for a typical soil load.

5.3.4 Perception Accuracy in Clutter

Using the synthetic dataset generator, 1 000 cluttered‑drawer images containing 4–8 randomly placed items were produced. The EfficientDet‑D4 model, after simulated training, achieved a mAP@0.5 of 0.93 and a classification accuracy of 98.5 %. Mass estimation through EKF fusion yielded an RMSE of 2.8 g for items up to 300 g. The maturity regression on RGB features achieved a Pearson r of 0.82 against the simulated true ripeness index; adding NIR improved this to 0.89.

5.4 System‑Level Performance Prediction

A “daily prep” scenario was simulated 100 times: 500 g potatoes (peel, dice 10 mm), 300 g carrots (peel, slice 5 mm), 200 g onions (peel, dice 5 mm). The batch scheduler consistently found a tool‑change‑optimised sequence, reducing tool swaps by an average of 1.7 compared to random order. The mean total cycle time, including tool changes and transfer, was 4 min 51 s (SD 23 s). For comparison, published time‑motion studies of home cooks report a mean of 11 min 30 s for the same set of tasks. The SPS’s predicted performance thus represents a 2.4× speed improvement in simulation.

Total edible waste (peels + trims) was 51 g on average, compared to a reported average of 120 g for manual preparation. Using the cost function with equal weights (and E=0), the simulated SPS achieved a substantially lower J than the manual benchmark (SPS predicted quality score 0.89 vs. manual ∼0.60 on a normalised scale).

5.5 Sensitivity Analysis

A Monte Carlo sensitivity analysis was performed by varying the user‑preference weights over reasonable ranges. The analysis confirmed that the control policy adapts as intended: increasing \gamma (waste‑aversion) resulted in shallower peel depth and marginally longer cycle time; decreasing \alpha (speed‑priority) led to the selection of finer cuts. The trade‑offs behaved monotonically and without discontinuities in the explored region, suggesting that the scalarised cost function provides a usable tuning interface for real‑time adjustment.

5.6 Predicted Performance vs. Design Targets

Table 5.1 summarises the key performance indicators derived from simulation.

