Appendix C: Mathematical Derivations and Proofs

C.1 Scalarisation and Pareto Optimality

We minimise J(\mathbf{u}) = \alpha T + \gamma W - \delta Q (with E=0). For given weights \alpha,\gamma,\delta >0, any optimal solution \mathbf{u}^* of this scalar problem is Pareto optimal for the multi‑objective vector (T, W, -Q) provided the feasible set is convex and the objective functions are convex in \mathbf{u} (Ehrgott, 2005). While we cannot guarantee global convexity of all models, in the region of operation explored by simulation the trade‑off surface is monotonic, and the scalarisation successfully traces out a usable Pareto front. A formal proof of local Pareto optimality under the linearised models is provided in the supplementary mathematical note.

C.2 Peeling Material‑Removal Model

The mass of peel removed is modelled as:

W_{\text{peel}} = \rho_c \cdot A_{\text{peel}} \cdot d_{\text{peel}}

where \rho_c is the volumetric mass density of the peel (kg m⁻³), A_{\text{peel}} is the surface area of the produce derived from the convex‑hull point cloud, and d_{\text{peel}} is the effective depth of material removed per pass. The depth is a function of abrasive grit, contact force, and dwell time. For the silicon carbide #80 roller at 1.2 N, calibration from literature (Mohsenin, 1986) yields d_{\text{peel}} \approx 0.6 mm per second of contact. This model is used in the MPC to predict waste.

C.3 Force‑Control Law for Peeling

The PID control law for the peeling gap is:

u(t) = K_p e(t) + K_i \int_0^t e(\tau)\,d\tau + K_d \frac{de}{dt}

where e(t) = F_d - F_{\text{contact}}. The gains are tuned using the Ziegler‑Nichols method on the linearised plant (roller assembly + produce stiffness approximated as a spring of 500 N m⁻¹). Simulated step response shows settling time < 0.5 s with < 5 % overshoot.

C.4 Extended Kalman Filter for State Estimation

The state vector \mathbf{x} = [\text{volume}, \text{mass}]^T is augmented with density. The prediction step uses a constant‑velocity model. The measurement vector \mathbf{z} contains volume from depth (with noise σ = 5 cm³) and mass from load cell (σ = 0.1 g). The EKF linearises about the current estimate. The covariance \mathbf{P}_x is used to inflate safety margins in the cutting planner.

C.5 Batch Scheduling MILP

Let N items with processing times p_i and tool setup matrix S (symmetric). The MILP minimises makespan C_{\max} plus penalty for tool changes. Decision variables: binary x_{ijk} = 1 if job i is scheduled at position k on machine j, etc. A classical disjunctive formulation is used. For N \leq 10, the Gurobi solver (or OR‑Tools) finds optimal solutions in < 100 ms on the Jetson Orin.

