Appendix J: Planned User Study Protocol

Objective: Compare SPS‑assisted preparation versus fully manual preparation on time, waste, cut quality, and perceived workload.

Participants: 30 adults (15 aged 20–40, 15 aged 65+ with mild mobility limitations, IRB‑approved recruitment).

Task: Prepare a standard meal prep set: 500 g potatoes (peel, dice 10 mm), 300 g carrots (peel, slice 5 mm), 200 g onions (peel, dice 5 mm).

Conditions (within‑subjects, counterbalanced):

· Condition A: Fully manual using standard kitchen tools.
· Condition B: SPS prototype (Phase 1). User loads whole vegetables, selects recipe, collects finished pieces.

Measures:

· Total task time (video‑coded).
· Edible waste mass (peels + off‑cuts collected and weighed).
· Cut uniformity (random sample of 20 pieces per type, measured with calliper; σ reported).
· NASA‑TLX workload questionnaire (post‑task).
· System Usability Scale (SUS) for Condition B.

Analysis: Paired t‑tests (or Wilcoxon) for time, waste, uniformity; descriptive statistics for NASA‑TLX and SUS. Significance level p < 0.05.

Status: Protocol designed; ethics approval will be sought prior to prototype availability.

